<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Hutchinson (governor)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1780">1780</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Hutchinson (1711–1780) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> merchant, historian and politician who served as lieutenant governor and then governor of the Province of Massachusetts Bay from 1771 to 1774. A loyalist, he enforced British policy, and his insistence that the taxed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> be landed led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> in 1773. He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1774 and died there in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-hutchinson-governor.jpg" alt="Thomas Hutchinson (governor)" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Truman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ThomasHutchinsonByEdwardTruman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hutchinson was born on 9 September 1711 in the North End of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, the fourth of twelve children of a wealthy merchant family descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-hutchinson">Anne Hutchinson</a>. He entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard College</a> at twelve, graduated in 1727 and by his own account turned a gift of five quintals of fish into between £400 and £500 by the age of 21. In 1734 he married Margaret Sanford, his third cousin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> selectman and to the General Court in 1737, he opposed the province's inflationary paper bills of credit, lost his seat in 1739 and later served as speaker from 1746 to 1749. In 1749 he used the British refund of about £180,000 in specie for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a> expedition to retire the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a>, a reform whose smooth success made him popular. Appointed lieutenant governor in 1758, he was also made chief justice by Governor Francis Bernard in place of James Otis Sr., which turned the Otises and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a> against him; his writs of assistance of 1761 deepened their hostility.</p><p>Although he warned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> against the Stamp Act, a crowd ransacked his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> mansion on the night of 26 August 1765, destroying its finishings and scattering his historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. He became acting governor when Bernard left in 1769, and after the Boston Massacre of 5 March 1770 he had the soldiers arrested and asked for the troops to withdraw to Castle William. His commission as governor arrived in March 1771.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As governor he moved the assembly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> and had his salary paid by the crown, measures his opponents denounced as arbitrary. In 1772 he told the assembly the colony was either wholly subject to Parliament or effectively independent. Letters he had written in the late 1760s, sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, were published by Samuel Adams in June 1773, and he was burned in effigy as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. In exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> he advised ministers and in 1776 anonymously published Strictures Upon the Declaration, a rebuttal of the Declaration of Independence. He died on 3 June 1780; the last volume of his History of the Province of Massachusetts Bay appeared in 1828.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Hutchinson (governor)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Thomas Hutchinson</b>, a native-born merchant, became the province's last civilian governor.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Province of Massachusetts Bay</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.36,-71.06&amp;year=1755" aria-label="Boston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Thomas Hutchinson (governor)"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4306 3918h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4306 3918h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.36,-71.06&amp;year=1755">What was here in 1755? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
